WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:16.379
There's scientific consensus on a lot of things:j
on climate change...

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:21.710
The best scientists in the world are all telling
us that our activities are changing the climate.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:23.880
...on HPV vaccine...

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:29.430
The majority of cervical cancer cases are
avoidable if we vaccinate against HPV.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:35.050
...and that deeply burying nuclear waste is
a good long term solution.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.900
So you would think that the public could agree
on these issues.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.640
In many ways, one would hope that that's true,
right?

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.220
The information that we learn about science
ends up getting filtered through our prior

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.970
beliefs, through our prior values, through
our religious views, our ideologies.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:54.290
And as a result, the exact same piece of scientific
information means very different things to

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:55.629
different people.

00:00:55.629 --> 00:01:00.409
Compliance for mandatory vaccinations is about
90 percent in the U.S.

00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:05.640
But the HPV vaccine, that protects against
cancer from a sexually transmitted virus,

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.730
is only mandatory in three places.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.850
You can frame HPV in two ways.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:16.340
You can either see this as a vaccine against
cancer and leave it at that.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.909
Or you can see it as a vaccine that, as some
people saw it as it was rolled out in Texas

00:01:20.909 --> 00:01:24.380
and elsewhere, that would lead to promiscuity.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.619
So why do we disagree on scientific consensus?

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:31.960
What's important to keep in mind here: when
we process new information, the way we as

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.799
human beings do that, we put them on mental
shelves.

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:38.880
I don't look at every piece of information
as if it's the first thing I've ever seen.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.149
I basically have in my head a number of mental
shelves that allow me to quickly categorize

00:01:43.149 --> 00:01:44.490
new information.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:49.450
So HPV vaccine— the HPV vaccine is a great
example because I can either put that on a

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:57.470
curing-cancer shelf or I can put that on a
sexual-intercourse-among-minors or among-people-who-we-think-shouldn't-have-that-yet

00:01:57.470 --> 00:01:59.579
shelf.

00:01:59.579 --> 00:02:02.890
Let's see how that works.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.479
We're going to introduce you to two composite
characters.

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:11.660
We're calling them Marcia and Marie and they
are proxies for a whole bunch of studies that

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:13.510
have looked at this issue.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:14.770
First meet Marcia.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:16.510
Hi, everyone.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:21.740
She's a working professional, college-educated,
she has two children, and she's scientifically

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:22.860
literate.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.200
Now meet Marie.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.810
Hi, everyone.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:31.330
She's a working professional, college-educated,
she has two children and she's also scientifically

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:32.330
literate.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.640
They are alike but so different.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.400
The government should stop telling people
how to live their lives.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.590
The government should put limits on the choices
of individuals so they don't get in the way

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:44.040
of what's good for society.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.290
It's not the government's business to make
laws that keep people from hurting themselves.

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:54.990
The government should do more to advance society's
goals, even if that means limiting the freedom

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:57.090
and choices of individuals.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:01.540
Their cultural and political outlooks on the
world differ, and researchers have labels

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:05.180
to describe that.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:09.090
Marcia has a hierarchical and individualist
view of the world.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.840
On climate change:

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.070
I really don't think there's a climate problem
and certainly not man-made.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:16.890
The HPV vaccine:

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:24.250
I mean, I don't know why my daughter has to
be vaccinated against a sexually transmitted

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:26.630
virus when she's only 12.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:29.510
And she thinks it's safe to bury nuclear waste.

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.250
It's going to be buried very deep underground.

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:34.900
I don't see why that's a problem.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:38.650
Marie's worldview is egalitarian and communitarian.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:40.630
On climate change:

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:44.910
Of course climate change is happening, and
we humans are making it worse.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.070
On the HPV vaccine:

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:51.560
As soon as I can protect my child from diseases,
the better.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.270
And on burying nuclear waste:

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:55.680
I'm really skeptical about burying it.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:04.200
There are earthquakes happening in places
we never thought.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.240
Their worldview affects how they assess risk.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:12.770
So "culture cognition" is a version of what
we've known in psychology for a long time

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:14.730
as "motivated reasoning".

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:18.949
And motivated reasoning says we, as human
beings, don't reason in a perfectly objective

00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:20.880
or neutral way.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.000
Scientific information may be fact-based or
neutral, but how we interpret that information

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.000
is not.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.030
We assimilate reality into our biased worldview.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:35.509
We don't adjust our worldview based on the
reality, but we adjust reality based on our

00:04:35.509 --> 00:04:36.509
worldview.

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:41.580
In fact, the more scientifically literate,
the more you stick to your beliefs.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.629
We protect our identities.

00:04:43.629 --> 00:04:45.150
It is all about identity protection.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:50.210
If I'm a strong conservative or strong liberal,
I don't want facts to constantly challenge

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:53.160
what I really hold deep down to be true.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:05:00.460
In an increasingly politically polarized world,
how do we fix this problem?

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:05.699
There's a consensus in this room that science
is good.

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:09.080
It's not just interesting but on the whole
it's good.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:14.349
But at the same time we all know that there's
a conversation about science taking part in

00:05:14.349 --> 00:05:17.169
our society that also is divisive.

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:19.460
That people get angry about science.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:26.110
That some of the divides in society that already
exist are enhanced and deepened over questions

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:27.370
related to science.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:30.810
And what we want to investigate tonight is
why is that?

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.879
And what can we do about that?

00:05:33.879 --> 00:05:34.960
Let's meet our panelists.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.900
Our first participant is a Professor of Law
and Psychology at Yale Law School who previously

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.620
clerked for Supreme Court Justice Thurgood
Marshall.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:47.110
He is one of the country's leading researchers
on risk perception and is part of the Cultural

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:52.860
Cognition Project where scholars study how
a cultural value shapes public risk perceptions

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.599
and related policy beliefs.

00:05:54.599 --> 00:06:06.229
A bona fide expert in the science of science
communication, please welcome Dan Kahan.

00:06:06.229 --> 00:06:09.520
Our next guest is a Nobel laureate, geneticist,
and cell biologist.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.919
He has been president of the Rockefeller University
here in New York.

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:17.360
He is now president of the Royal Society of
London— sorry, he stopped doing that in

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:18.759
2015, but he was.

00:06:18.759 --> 00:06:22.039
And a member of the U.K. Prime Minister's
Scientific Advisory Committee.

00:06:22.039 --> 00:06:25.599
At present, he is the director of the Francis
Crick Institute in London.

00:06:25.599 --> 00:06:32.539
Please welcome Sir Paul Nurse.

00:06:32.539 --> 00:06:38.599
Our next guest is responsible for a federal
agency that has a budget of $7.5 billion dollars

00:06:38.599 --> 00:06:40.629
devoted to science.

00:06:40.629 --> 00:06:42.919
Before that, she was president of Purdue University.

00:06:42.919 --> 00:06:47.789
She's been a group leader at Los Alamos National
Lab, an astrophysicist, she became NASA's

00:06:47.789 --> 00:06:49.870
first female chief scientist.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:07:02.189
Please welcome the director of the National
Science Foundation, France Córdova.

00:07:02.189 --> 00:07:07.589
And our final panelist is a Professor of Physics
and Mathematics at Columbia University.

00:07:07.589 --> 00:07:12.110
Known for groundbreaking discoveries in the
area of superstring theory, he is a bestselling

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:17.210
author, has hosted two award-winning series
on NOVA, his TED talk, "Making Sense of String

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:22.509
Theory", has been viewed over 4 million times,
and a co-founder of the World Science Festival

00:07:22.509 --> 00:07:24.059
which is now in its tenth year.

00:07:24.059 --> 00:07:32.279
Please welcome Brian Greene.

00:07:32.279 --> 00:07:37.469
So on this question of polarization of science
I want to go straight to Sir Paul.

00:07:37.469 --> 00:07:45.720
You, last year, produced a BBC program called
"Science Under Attack", which I strongly recommend.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.560
Science created our modern world.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:55.740
So I want to understand why science appears
to be under such attack, and whether we scientists

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:57.740
are partly to blame.

00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:02.139
But what are the battlelines of this attack
that you were talking about?

00:08:02.139 --> 00:08:10.690
Well, I think we do have an issue that is
of significance and that is when science comes

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:18.750
up against politics, ideology, and religion,
then we can get quite a potent brew.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:23.339
And in certain issues— and we saw them in
the introduction, climate change would be

00:08:23.339 --> 00:08:30.900
one, vaccination, GMOs— these are particularly
toxic in the public mind.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:39.080
And really what I wanted to communicate with
that program was that we should initially

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:43.089
look at the science first, if we can do that.

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:47.250
Because obviously, psychologically, we're
looking at everything at the same time.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:54.350
But we should look at the science first rather
than first of all take an ideological or political

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:59.050
position— and again, we saw that with the
two individuals you had up there— and then

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:04.480
cherry pick the science data to fit with your
ideology, your religion, your politics, or

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:05.589
whatever.

00:09:05.589 --> 00:09:12.100
That is the wrong way to do things and that
will lead to a very poor use of science.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:16.720
And I think that's a critical point that we
should try and address, perhaps this evening.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:21.670
Do you need to be a scientist to know how
to do that?

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:30.410
You need to be a scientist to produce information,
but we need to have a culture that will recognize

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:32.420
that that is the right way to do that.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:38.310
And of course, what that means is that we
need to have a society which respects the

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:44.370
process of science— that's a little bit
abstract and perhaps difficult to win hearts

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:51.800
and minds on— and we also need to have a
trust in the scientists who are carrying it

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:52.800
out.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:54.250
So it's a trust in a process.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:56.149
It's a trust in the scientists.

00:09:56.149 --> 00:09:59.070
But we have to recognize scientists are human
beings.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:02.570
They are not perfect and they will make mistakes.

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:05.970
Everything isn't perfect that they do.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:09.269
And sometimes there's even fraud, for example.

00:10:09.269 --> 00:10:15.670
We also have to understand that not all science
is on a completely secure foundation.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:20.050
If we look at Newton's laws of physics they've
been around for three centuries, have been

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:26.740
tested repeatedly, we can tell where a planet
will be within centimeters with great accuracy.

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:34.220
If, on the other hand, you ask a scientist
whether the next Ebola epidemic is going to

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:39.370
kill a hundred million people or one million
people, that's a much more difficult question

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:40.640
to answer.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:46.509
And science is often, I prefer to use the
term "tentative knowledge", knowledge that

00:10:46.509 --> 00:10:48.350
is evolving and developing.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:53.449
Now if we have those two things together,
that is tentative knowledge and the human

00:10:53.449 --> 00:11:00.540
frailty that can produce incorrect science,
that leaves gaps spaces which can be exploited

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:06.600
by those with political and ideological and
even religical opinions to cherry pick what

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:07.790
they want.

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:13.069
And we need to understand all of that process
if we're going to get this right.

00:11:13.069 --> 00:11:18.670
France Córdova, I want to…You graduated
from Stanford not planning to go into science

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:19.810
at all.

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:22.190
You graduated with a degree in what?

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:23.380
English.

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:25.750
English Literature.

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:27.930
So what happened?

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:35.720
What happened to me is I was inspired by a
public television show to become a scientist.

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:40.510
I was inspired by the moon landing, of course
that was very dramatic and all.

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:46.940
But it was really the public television show
where they featured scientists talking about

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:52.709
these strange new stars they had discovered
called "neutron stars", and it so captivated

00:11:52.709 --> 00:11:59.550
me and I thought, 'What would I ultimately
like to do and give myself over to growing

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:00.550
up?'

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:02.040
And I thought, 'That's what I want to be.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.360
I want to be a scientist.'

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:08.529
So I just went in to— I just knocked on
the doors, actually of some of the people

00:12:08.529 --> 00:12:11.690
who were featured in that movie at MIT.

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:15.250
So you make this transition from the world
of not being a scientist into the world of

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:16.740
being a scientist.

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:19.589
You were that layperson who became the professional.

00:12:19.589 --> 00:12:20.630
That's right.

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:24.300
What was the thing that you needed to absorb
about how science works that you think maybe

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.959
isn't so obvious to regular folks?

00:12:26.959 --> 00:12:33.420
I always think of science as an exploration,
of just going to the next frontier, the next

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:37.279
step, and that's the unknown.

00:12:37.279 --> 00:12:44.720
And that's a constantly evolving process so
I wouldn't expect science to yield- I expect

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:51.250
it to reveal itself little by little as one
explores it just like any new frontier does.

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:53.860
But never would you know everything.

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:59.310
The universe is so vast and there's so many
things that we have no knowledge of.

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:05.779
You're constantly trying to penetrate and
go deeper into that unknown.

00:13:05.779 --> 00:13:07.090
That's the part that drives me.

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:09.640
I think it drives the public as well.

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:14.399
I think they have more curiosity than we give
them credit for.

00:13:14.399 --> 00:13:16.750
Well, speaking of public, let me take it to
Brian.

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:20.879
You really are— and I mean this in a very
positive way— a popularizer of science with

00:13:20.879 --> 00:13:22.730
the television programs that you do.

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:27.009
But that means that you need to find a language
to speak to people who may not understand

00:13:27.009 --> 00:13:28.150
the subtleties.

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:33.750
And it's sort of the same question to you,
and again it's bouncing off of what Paul said,

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:38.160
whether people really grasp what the scientific
process is about.

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:39.160
Yeah.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:41.899
So I'm going to give you two answers: one
from the standpoint of the science itself,

00:13:41.899 --> 00:13:45.490
and the other from communicating the ideas.

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:51.910
And I think from the standpoint of the science,
I think there is a general resistance to being

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:55.310
in a place of uncertainty.

00:13:55.310 --> 00:14:01.680
And what happens is when there is a glimmer
of uncertainty, it turns into a general impression

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:05.569
that the scientists don't know what they're
talking about, and therefore I don't really

00:14:05.569 --> 00:14:09.160
need to pay attention to what's going on right
now because they haven't figured it out and

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:10.350
they don't know what's going on.

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:15.029
I think a good example of that - which takes
us a little bit away from the political sphere,

00:14:15.029 --> 00:14:19.730
which I think makes a nice little laboratory
setting— there is an article in Scientific

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:25.350
American in February not to do with climate
change or vaccines, but having to do with

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:26.949
cosmology.

00:14:26.949 --> 00:14:33.430
And the article is basically challenging the
prevailing view of cosmology.

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:39.509
And in response to that, a number of scientists
who believe strongly in the prevailing view

00:14:39.509 --> 00:14:44.040
wrote a letter to the editor expressing their
view.

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:46.920
So this has now come out as a disagreement.

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:51.660
And what I expected would happen has happened,
because I've spoken to a number of people

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:57.430
during this festival who have come up to me
with the general impression, 'Ah the cosmology

00:14:57.430 --> 00:14:58.810
is now falling apart.

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:02.339
Nobody knows anything that's going on when
it comes to cosmology.'

00:15:02.339 --> 00:15:06.579
And the fact is, the only reason why we're
having this controversy is because we understand

00:15:06.579 --> 00:15:11.959
things so spectacularly well that now we can
ask the kinds of questions that we couldn't

00:15:11.959 --> 00:15:13.269
even ask 20 years ago.

00:15:13.269 --> 00:15:18.139
And it's in those fine details that certain
issues are starting to bubble up and different

00:15:18.139 --> 00:15:19.939
perspectives are coming to the fore.

00:15:19.939 --> 00:15:23.350
So it's a beautiful example of the scientific
process.

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:27.839
Where now, through observation and experiment,
we have a deeper handle on what's going on,

00:15:27.839 --> 00:15:29.450
allowing us to go further into ideas.

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:35.060
But when you get it out there into the general
public— say through Scientific American—

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:40.839
the impression that it leaves is radically
different from the actual state of the field.

00:15:40.839 --> 00:15:44.860
Dan Kahan— first of all, tell us what you
do for a living.

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:47.050
I'm not sure I do anything for a living.

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:53.829
I haven't worked since June 30th, 1993, which
is the last day before my first academic appointment

00:15:53.829 --> 00:15:57.279
became operative.

00:15:57.279 --> 00:16:04.389
But I guess the work I do is to study science
communication and risk perception, but to

00:16:04.389 --> 00:16:10.439
try to understand the psychological dynamics
that account for the kind of political polarization

00:16:10.439 --> 00:16:13.220
that was featured in our opening.

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:19.370
And then to try to come up with mechanisms
or strategies that can make that less disruptive

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:22.769
of what would otherwise be the great benefit
we get from all we know.

00:16:22.769 --> 00:16:29.290
So what are the ruling theories on, again,
the situation that Paul laid out?

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:36.550
One thing is, I don't think we should understand
this to be a consequence of limitations in

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:43.560
people's ability to understand science and
maybe problems they have with uncertainty.

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:49.139
One of the most striking things about this
research is that the higher people score on

00:16:49.139 --> 00:16:55.550
any measure of science comprehension and critical
thinking, the more intensely they're processing

00:16:55.550 --> 00:17:01.259
the information in a way that conforms it
to the beliefs that predominate in their group.

00:17:01.259 --> 00:17:02.259
Can you repeat that?

00:17:02.259 --> 00:17:10.520
So the people who are highest in science literacy,
the people who are highest in critical reasoning—they're

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:17.380
the ones who most aggressively construe information
about these contested issues in a way that

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:21.880
conduces to beliefs that are in line with
the ones in their group.

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:25.220
So these aren't people who are just confused
about science.

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:32.690
I mean it's actually a tragic thing that they're
using their reason in that way to try to understand

00:17:32.690 --> 00:17:37.210
the evidence and the facts in ways that confirm
what their group believes.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:41.090
But the problem isn't any kind of ignorance
about science at all.

00:17:41.090 --> 00:17:46.059
When this happens, people who have very acute
reasoning processes - they're applying their

00:17:46.059 --> 00:17:49.770
cognitive resources to get the answers that
support their group.

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:52.760
I want to come back to the specifics of your
research.

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:55.500
But does that surprise everybody else?

00:17:55.500 --> 00:18:00.480
I do find it slightly surprising, although
it aligns with certain things.

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:05.760
That would make a lot of sense to me when
the scientific issues directly impact on things

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:07.470
that have relevance to our lives.

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:12.919
Because then, as you say, if a certain kind
of scientific insight might lead to a policy

00:18:12.919 --> 00:18:16.500
that goes against how you want the world to
be, I can imagine that all sorts of other

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:21.830
issues bubble up and cause you to— as we've
discussed before— cherry pick an order that

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:25.190
it won't yield that kind of an outcome.

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:30.850
But have you done studies where people are
presented with scientific issues that don't

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:32.110
have that?

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:36.870
Well, and we should be realistic about this.

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:38.640
Most issues don't have this problem.

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:45.650
There are many, many more science insights
about risk that don't involve this perverse

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:50.730
effect of people with different values forming
different views at all, much less people who

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:56.559
have different values and the most developed
capacity for science comprehension polarizing

00:18:56.559 --> 00:18:58.039
all the more.

00:18:58.039 --> 00:19:04.510
These are issues that are pathological, both
in the sense of being bad but also being rare.

00:19:04.510 --> 00:19:11.740
And if you don't understand the great class
of issues that don't have this feature, you'll

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:15.770
never actually be able to have good hypotheses
about what causes it.

00:19:15.770 --> 00:19:18.700
And maybe just one quick example on this.

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:24.679
This affects science in a really consequential
way and we should be concerned about it.

00:19:24.679 --> 00:19:27.409
But these dynamics are not specific to science.

00:19:27.409 --> 00:19:35.430
If I took—was it Marie and Marsha?—and
I showed them a video of a protest and I told

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:41.610
them that the political protest was at an
abortion clinic, then they would come out

00:19:41.610 --> 00:19:43.980
with very different views about what they
saw happening.

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:48.780
There was somebody blocking somebody, there
was somebody hitting somebody else over the

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:49.870
head with a sign.

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:56.250
If I them it was a military recruitment center
protest, then they would flip around and see

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:57.250
exactly the opposite.

00:19:57.250 --> 00:20:01.500
I mean, I don't know about them as individuals,
but groups of people have their values.

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:07.850
So when facts become entangled in these group
identities so that positions are like badges

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:14.190
of identity and vouchers of loyalty to the
group, people disagree on the facts.

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:16.059
But that's not limited to science.

00:20:16.059 --> 00:20:21.170
But the implication of what you're saying
is that there is no solution in just giving

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:22.260
people the facts.

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:23.380
That that's not going to work.

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:29.320
Well in just giving people the facts, they're
going to selectively credit and discredit

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:33.770
the information in the patterns that reinforce
their group beliefs.

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:37.990
And the better they are at critical reasoning,
the more successfully they're going to do

00:20:37.990 --> 00:20:38.990
that.

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:41.380
That's not the information that they're missing.

00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:43.110
Are you personally immune from that?

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:45.310
Yes I am, personally.

00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:51.179
I did a bone marrow transplant from Brian—
thank you for that.

00:20:51.179 --> 00:20:54.529
No, I don't think I'm immune from it.

00:20:54.529 --> 00:20:59.659
And I think too if I put it this way, probably
most people recognize, 'this could be happening

00:20:59.659 --> 00:21:01.070
to me on certain issues.'

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:02.760
But you don't know when.

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:06.030
We say that people are cherry picking, but
they're not doing it consciously.

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:09.590
They're doing it unconsciously.

00:21:09.590 --> 00:21:14.850
Paul, again to go back to the documentary
that you did— you talked with a couple of

00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:20.230
activists in the U.K. who have taken the position
that climate science is not proven, never

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:24.210
established, and in fact is a very dubious
proposition.

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:31.830
And you sat there— and it's clear what you
think the truth is— and you sat there and

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:35.580
you let them tell you that you were wrong.

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:39.900
And one of them certainly was not a scientist
at all.

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:45.450
One of them was a journalist who, when you
asked him what studies he read, said, "I don't

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:46.450
read studies.

00:21:46.450 --> 00:21:49.299
I read reviews of studies that other people
do."

00:21:49.299 --> 00:21:57.190
And so you had a very, very pleasant exchange
in which you allowed it to get across that

00:21:57.190 --> 00:22:00.309
the two of you had equal standing in that
conversation.

00:22:00.309 --> 00:22:04.030
Well, do you have equal standing in that conversation?

00:22:04.030 --> 00:22:10.539
And did you have to go outside and scream
after you with talked that guy?

00:22:10.539 --> 00:22:18.130
Well you know, I was rather wicked in that
conversation because I realized I was dealing

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:24.690
with an individual who did not believe the
climate was changing, certainly didn't believe

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:28.220
human activity had any role in it.

00:22:28.220 --> 00:22:34.270
He came at it through his own politics and
ideology, which I'll talk a little bit about

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:36.210
in a moment.

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:39.559
And he had supreme self-confidence.

00:22:39.559 --> 00:22:45.060
And he was so used to people shouting at him—
and then he could get into a fight— but,

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:50.230
as you saw, I just listened politely until
he hung himself.

00:22:50.230 --> 00:22:55.440
And I think sometimes the consensual position
can be criticized when in fact, it is most

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:58.970
likely to be the correct position.

00:22:58.970 --> 00:23:00.990
Yep.

00:23:00.990 --> 00:23:03.010
Um.

00:23:03.010 --> 00:23:04.289
Should we talk about— should we talk about
Climategate?

00:23:04.289 --> 00:23:05.970
Because I don’t accept your analogy, really.

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:13.870
I think it’s uh… [laughs]

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:20.679
What are folks like you scientists supposed
to do in the face of people who are not scientists,

00:23:20.679 --> 00:23:24.970
who perhaps do or do not understand the scientific
process?

00:23:24.970 --> 00:23:29.659
Who simply tell you that you are wrong and
that there's a controversy?

00:23:29.659 --> 00:23:32.130
Two sides of a story.

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:33.410
I do have some thoughts about this.

00:23:33.410 --> 00:23:39.290
I think the first position that scientists
should take is actually to listen to what

00:23:39.290 --> 00:23:44.870
they have to say and see whether there is
a way that you can reason and discuss it.

00:23:44.870 --> 00:23:50.190
You shouldn't automatically assume that it
isn't possible to have dialogue and debate.

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:53.710
In the issue of climate change, you can understand
why there's a range of views.

00:23:53.710 --> 00:23:56.649
It's a very complex subject.

00:23:56.649 --> 00:24:00.159
It's one that is very data-ridden.

00:24:00.159 --> 00:24:01.720
The models are difficult to do.

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:09.400
There are all these holes that people can
occupy because there are gaps in the science.

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:15.000
And what has emerged there in climate change
is something that I think is really quite

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:16.000
interesting.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:20.650
Because there is some debate about the science.

00:24:20.650 --> 00:24:27.400
But what I think influences it most is, what
do you do if there really is climate change

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:31.160
being caused by human intervention?

00:24:31.160 --> 00:24:35.680
Because what you do is very significant for
individuals' beliefs.

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:41.200
Because the only way you can deal with it
is something like the Paris Agreement, which

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:51.140
requires agreement across countries, top-down,
and doesn't allow the individual or the company

00:24:51.140 --> 00:24:55.370
or even the state or the country to find their
own way.

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:57.940
It requires collective action.

00:24:57.940 --> 00:25:03.260
And that collective action is an anathema
to those of a certain political bent and a

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:05.050
certain ideology.

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:10.820
On the other hand, for another section of
society it's actually the way they think and

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:14.740
the way they like to think about issues.

00:25:14.740 --> 00:25:20.769
That means that the science problem turns
into a political problem because the minds

00:25:20.769 --> 00:25:25.500
are lined up really on politics rather than
the science.

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:32.780
And then you get all the dirtiness of political
discussion being thrown at the science.

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:38.490
And I think that, from my amateurish view
of this, has been the real problem here.

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:45.230
It's become a political issue and the science
is refracted through those politics.

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:51.100
We are talking about polarization, but there's
actually evidence that there's almost a sort

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:56.350
of vast silent majority of citizens who are
behind science.

00:25:56.350 --> 00:26:02.669
I mean, there were surveys done where the
question was whether the U.S. federal government

00:26:02.669 --> 00:26:04.710
should even fund science.

00:26:04.710 --> 00:26:08.580
And as you can see, that orange in the graphic
is overwhelming support for science on the

00:26:08.580 --> 00:26:10.730
part of the general public.

00:26:10.730 --> 00:26:16.179
And then a second question was asked about
whether there's enough money going to science.

00:26:16.179 --> 00:26:20.990
And again surprisingly, the majority of the
public so there's not enough money being spent

00:26:20.990 --> 00:26:21.990
on science.

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:23.269
Can I make one point?

00:26:23.269 --> 00:26:29.730
We should really be cautious not to characterize
this kind of disagreement as the people who

00:26:29.730 --> 00:26:31.720
believe in science and the people who don't.

00:26:31.720 --> 00:26:32.720
How should we?

00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:39.450
First of all, everybody— as those graphics
were helping us to see— have very positive

00:26:39.450 --> 00:26:41.029
views of science.

00:26:41.029 --> 00:26:47.549
They're just as confused or just as much in
a state of polarization over what the majority

00:26:47.549 --> 00:26:53.600
of scientists say or over scientific consensus,
as they are on the facts of whether global

00:26:53.600 --> 00:26:59.320
warming is happening or whether deep geologic
isolation of nuclear waste is a sensible idea.

00:26:59.320 --> 00:27:05.539
Those are examples that we used in an experiment
to test whether people could recognize scientific

00:27:05.539 --> 00:27:06.539
expertise.

00:27:06.539 --> 00:27:11.659
And it turns out that people will recognize
a scientist as an expert if that person has

00:27:11.659 --> 00:27:16.840
the same view as their group on an issue like
deep geologic isolation of nuclear waste or

00:27:16.840 --> 00:27:17.840
climate change.

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:19.580
So they're engaged in a kind of biased sampling.

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:24.920
But at the end, both sides say that scientific
consensus is on their side.

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:30.169
This is like nations going to war saying,
"God is on our side."

00:27:30.169 --> 00:27:32.220
Nobody is out there saying, "Screw the scientists."

00:27:32.220 --> 00:27:33.809
Not in the general public, at least.

00:27:33.809 --> 00:27:38.139
We have another graph that takes a look—
in fact— at public trust, I believe is the

00:27:38.139 --> 00:27:44.039
topic, in scientists and medical scientists,
which may or may not come up—

00:27:44.039 --> 00:27:47.760
The GSS, the General Social Survey has been
doing this for 40 years.

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:50.580
Science is always second out of 13 institutions.

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:53.840
Sometimes it's the military, sometimes it's
the medical profession but it's always been

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:57.500
second and it's been second for both Republicans
and Democrats.

00:27:57.500 --> 00:28:01.309
Republicans trusted the science community
during the Reagan administration more than

00:28:01.309 --> 00:28:03.360
trusted the executive branch.

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:07.130
People who were religious in nature trust
science more than they say they trust organized

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:08.130
clergy.

00:28:08.130 --> 00:28:12.710
So we're not in a society where people are
questioning, but they have a hard time figuring

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:16.250
out what scientists believe on some of these
issues.

00:28:16.250 --> 00:28:21.740
Brian, you're in primarily physics and the
physical sciences.

00:28:21.740 --> 00:28:26.450
Is it a different kind of - do you face these
kinds of controversies in the same sort of

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:27.450
way?

00:28:27.450 --> 00:28:29.830
I know you told the story you told before,
but does it get as politicized?

00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:36.760
Well no, I feel that we're in a fortunate
position because most of the people are spending

00:28:36.760 --> 00:28:41.059
their energy fighting against these folks
in the biology and climate area.

00:28:41.059 --> 00:28:43.120
They haven't gotten to physics yet.

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:45.029
Maybe that happens in the future.

00:28:45.029 --> 00:28:52.220
And it's largely because what we're doing
doesn't have a direct impact on policy, that

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:58.760
has something where one can tangibly see it'll
affect their lives in one way or another.

00:28:58.760 --> 00:29:06.240
And aligned with that, relevant to the question
you asked before, again, in terms of quantum

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:11.620
mechanics and general relativity, say— these
are complex and rich and deep subjects.

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:15.290
But in a way, they're very simple in that
there aren't a lot of moving parts.

00:29:15.290 --> 00:29:17.929
It comes to climate change, goodness gracious.

00:29:17.929 --> 00:29:19.279
Let me give you an example.

00:29:19.279 --> 00:29:22.919
I've been asked to be on programs to talk
about climate change.

00:29:22.919 --> 00:29:24.240
I don't do it.

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:25.380
You know why don't do it?

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:30.830
I don't feel like I have a grasp of the science
at a deep enough level where if there's somebody

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:34.490
really good from the other side, that they
won't be able to destroy me.

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:40.289
Whereas I know with almost certainty that
that will not happen if it's a discussion

00:29:40.289 --> 00:29:43.710
about quantum mechanics or unified theory
or general relativity.

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:49.170
So I feel completely comfortable discussing
that with anybody regardless of how skeptical

00:29:49.170 --> 00:29:50.549
they might be.

00:29:50.549 --> 00:29:55.851
But climate is such a complex subject that
even for a scientist, it's difficult.

00:29:55.851 --> 00:29:58.419
I actually tried to start to read the literature.

00:29:58.419 --> 00:30:04.630
I said, "Let me educate myself once and for
all so I can just handle all of these climate

00:30:04.630 --> 00:30:06.210
issues straight out."

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:11.890
And I start to read the papers, and a week
or two in, I'm like, "I can't do this.

00:30:11.890 --> 00:30:15.980
This is going to take up my entire life because
it is so complicated."

00:30:15.980 --> 00:30:20.120
Now again, that's not to say that I am a skeptic.

00:30:20.120 --> 00:30:26.159
It's to say that there's so many moving parts
that to be able to rebut any argument that

00:30:26.159 --> 00:30:32.200
comes at you requires a level of understanding
and a depth of understanding that's difficult

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:33.200
to achieve.

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:39.659
Whereas in the physical sciences, go through
graduate school and you're there.

00:30:39.659 --> 00:30:41.630
So that's the difference.

00:30:41.630 --> 00:30:43.370
Paul, take climate change then.

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:50.370
If it is so complicated that even the brilliant
Brian Greene stopped after a couple of weeks,

00:30:50.370 --> 00:30:54.960
what is everybody else supposed to do in figuring
out what their position is?

00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:59.640
Well as I was saying about climate change
because I'm with Brian, I mean it's a complex

00:30:59.640 --> 00:31:00.640
subject.

00:31:00.640 --> 00:31:04.690
There's many holes and it takes a lot of navigation.

00:31:04.690 --> 00:31:13.299
I think what we have to do if we can is to
have, if we can, a more mature discussion

00:31:13.299 --> 00:31:18.809
about science in society and with our political
leaders.

00:31:18.809 --> 00:31:25.909
We have to somehow communicate the strengths
of science, the weaknesses of science, this

00:31:25.909 --> 00:31:31.780
tentative knowledge point that I was saying,
that we cannot always say things with certainty.

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:37.570
I think one problem in school is that science
is often taught with complete certainty.

00:31:37.570 --> 00:31:39.890
And that is a real difficulty.

00:31:39.890 --> 00:31:45.409
Another problem with school is that we don't
focus enough on the process of science and

00:31:45.409 --> 00:31:46.659
how you do good science.

00:31:46.659 --> 00:31:49.610
And so I think we have to start there in education.

00:31:49.610 --> 00:31:57.610
Give us the 45 second process of science—
bottom line— that people can take home.

00:31:57.610 --> 00:32:01.491
I think I prefer to answer the question in
terms of, what are the attributes of good

00:32:01.491 --> 00:32:02.700
science?

00:32:02.700 --> 00:32:09.350
And the attributes of good science are respect
for good data observation and reproducible

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:10.640
results.

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:14.269
We are an empirical activity.

00:32:14.269 --> 00:32:19.190
We have to look at the evidence, so we're
relying on good observation.

00:32:19.190 --> 00:32:25.990
The second is rational argument - simply applying
the logic, trying to be as objective as you

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:32.990
can having recognized that we are human beings
pushed around by our feelings and by our other

00:32:32.990 --> 00:32:33.990
beliefs.

00:32:33.990 --> 00:32:36.149
So rational argument is the second.

00:32:36.149 --> 00:32:41.350
The third is skepticism, particularly of your
own ideas.

00:32:41.350 --> 00:32:44.180
So you attack your own ideas.

00:32:44.180 --> 00:32:50.269
And finally you go through a process that,
with those observations, you come up with

00:32:50.269 --> 00:32:54.889
a hypothesis, an idea to explain the phenomena
of interest and then you attack it.

00:32:54.889 --> 00:32:58.170
You don't actually try to defend it, you attack
it.

00:32:58.170 --> 00:33:03.659
And if it survives sufficient attacks, then
you can start thinking this could represent

00:33:03.659 --> 00:33:04.659
it.

00:33:04.659 --> 00:33:05.659
Do you realize how vital that is?

00:33:05.659 --> 00:33:11.539
Scientists are the most skeptical people around,
and yet there is a line that's out there in

00:33:11.539 --> 00:33:15.380
the public that somehow they'll defend a perspective
to the death.

00:33:15.380 --> 00:33:21.809
No, we try to kill it every step of the way
and only if it survives every sledgehammer

00:33:21.809 --> 00:33:26.159
that we throw at it, are we willing to believe
it or have confidence in it.

00:33:26.159 --> 00:33:28.179
That's a very different process.

00:33:28.179 --> 00:33:34.559
But here's one thing: I think that the analogy,
Brian, between you and the member of the public

00:33:34.559 --> 00:33:35.620
breaks down.

00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:40.549
They're not going to understand anything on
their own by pouring over it.

00:33:40.549 --> 00:33:45.440
And they're going to have to accept much more
is known by science then they could possibly

00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:46.440
understand.

00:33:46.440 --> 00:33:49.630
They don't become experts in any area of science
inable to do that.

00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:53.429
They become experts at figuring out who knows
what about what.

00:33:53.429 --> 00:33:58.299
And they're not even hearing from scientists,
they're immersed in social networks.

00:33:58.299 --> 00:34:03.429
And those do, generally speaking, a very good
job of leading them to what the truth is.

00:34:03.429 --> 00:34:06.980
But when they're in social groups that are
at odds with each other, they're just doing

00:34:06.980 --> 00:34:11.260
the same thing that they ordinarily do to
figure out what science knows and really,

00:34:11.260 --> 00:34:12.970
there's no alternative to it.

00:34:12.970 --> 00:34:17.690
I think that we perhaps don't use enough is
"trust".

00:34:17.690 --> 00:34:24.419
When I say we need to have a more mature discussion—
with society and politics— of course they

00:34:24.419 --> 00:34:27.929
cannot understand the science, of course they
can't understand what Brian does.

00:34:27.929 --> 00:34:32.480
But they can trust Brian, and they can trust
the process.

00:34:32.480 --> 00:34:37.590
And I think we need to do the following things:
we need to focus on developing trust in science

00:34:37.590 --> 00:34:43.579
and trust in scientists, and I think in practical
terms we need to work with our politicians.

00:34:43.579 --> 00:34:46.490
When they disagree, you need to have courteous
debate.

00:34:46.490 --> 00:34:52.680
I don't mean lobbying, which actually I think
is perhaps too prevalent, particularly in

00:34:52.680 --> 00:34:53.680
the U.S.

00:34:53.680 --> 00:34:54.680
Because what do scientists do?

00:34:54.680 --> 00:34:56.960
They go and see their politicians when they
want more money.

00:34:56.960 --> 00:34:59.670
Otherwise they take no notice of them.

00:34:59.670 --> 00:35:04.520
What we need to do is to make friends with
the politicians and get the politicians working

00:35:04.520 --> 00:35:10.329
with scientists to provide leadership in these
sorts of issues.

00:35:10.329 --> 00:35:16.000
Do you see a change in this trust issue over
the course of your career?

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:17.500
I want to take that to France.

00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:20.369
Well there's a lot more social media now.

00:35:20.369 --> 00:35:24.820
And as Dan was saying, there's a lot more
places to get one’s information and there's

00:35:24.820 --> 00:35:30.420
a lot more so-called "experts" around.

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:36.020
So that's certainly— people can always pull
up or grab on to something to affirm their

00:35:36.020 --> 00:35:37.020
beliefs.

00:35:37.020 --> 00:35:42.430
But there's a vast number of people in the
middle—the bulk of of humanity— that is

00:35:42.430 --> 00:35:43.599
curious.

00:35:43.599 --> 00:35:50.510
Even if you have a belief, if you're Marie
or Marcia in the video, if you're a curious

00:35:50.510 --> 00:35:59.079
Maria or Marcia, you will be open to having
your thinking changed with the right narratives

00:35:59.079 --> 00:36:03.010
and stories about the research that's being
done and so on.

00:36:03.010 --> 00:36:07.230
And those are the people that I want to address.

00:36:07.230 --> 00:36:11.790
I'd like to give you an example of something
that I did about a week ago.

00:36:11.790 --> 00:36:20.670
I accompanied nine congressmen and their staff
to Greenland—to see the Arctic ice sheet

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:22.370
in Greenland.

00:36:22.370 --> 00:36:29.120
And I think everybody here can appreciate
that since it's covered by ice, the whole

00:36:29.120 --> 00:36:38.060
thing, that we're talking about glaciers receding
and the sea ice melting and environmental

00:36:38.060 --> 00:36:49.180
change on a very big scale that is noticeable
over the period of just years and the decade.

00:36:49.180 --> 00:36:58.250
And are— I think the approach was very much
just to go and see and show them our facilities

00:36:58.250 --> 00:37:03.780
there, the kind of research we're doing, and
also with other agencies.

00:37:03.780 --> 00:37:09.810
And when people can see for themselves—
and these were congressmen that cross the

00:37:09.810 --> 00:37:17.510
spectrum of belief about certain things, and
from both sides of the aisle.

00:37:17.510 --> 00:37:25.050
And I think it was really revealing, without
being at all heavy-handed.

00:37:25.050 --> 00:37:33.440
Just going to the different sites and showing
the ice core bore down more than a mile deep

00:37:33.440 --> 00:37:44.040
and taking samples of emissions of gases down
150,000 years ago, to the research on the

00:37:44.040 --> 00:37:47.340
ice and the glaciers and so forth.

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:50.280
They all asked a lot of very, very good questions.

00:37:50.280 --> 00:37:57.520
And I think that's kind of the approach that
I think of scientists just doing more of,

00:37:57.520 --> 00:38:04.680
is inviting elected officials to universities
to see their laboratories, meet their students,

00:38:04.680 --> 00:38:10.640
see the kind of research they're doing, have
them ask the questions instead of putting

00:38:10.640 --> 00:38:16.170
things and ideas in front of them and feeding
them, is let them feed themselves by being

00:38:16.170 --> 00:38:17.829
exposed more.

00:38:17.829 --> 00:38:21.790
Do you think any minds changed on that trip?

00:38:21.790 --> 00:38:25.060
It's an excellent question and I don't know.

00:38:25.060 --> 00:38:29.190
Sometimes one prepares one's mind to change.

00:38:29.190 --> 00:38:35.130
I'm not sure that we saw somebody jump up
and have an aha moment.

00:38:35.130 --> 00:38:39.770
Although people did say, "I need to go back
and read more about this.

00:38:39.770 --> 00:38:41.420
I need to learn more."

00:38:41.420 --> 00:38:46.609
An interesting distinction between the climate
change discussion here and the discussion

00:38:46.609 --> 00:38:53.369
over genetically modified food in the U.K.
is that here, the challenge to the consensus

00:38:53.369 --> 00:38:54.810
tends to come from the right.

00:38:54.810 --> 00:38:59.619
But Paul, tell us what's happening with the
question of GMO food in the U.K.

00:38:59.619 --> 00:39:02.369
Well it's exactly as you said.

00:39:02.369 --> 00:39:10.089
In the U.K., climate change and the man-made
cause is more or less accepted.

00:39:10.089 --> 00:39:15.100
But there's been much greater resistance with
GM crops compared with the U.S.

00:39:15.100 --> 00:39:20.540
And this is really quite interesting and I'm
not sure I can answer it in the general way

00:39:20.540 --> 00:39:24.550
that you're asking, I can only describe what
sort of happened.

00:39:24.550 --> 00:39:31.260
The first thing that happened is that Monsanto—that's
the company who first marketed engineered

00:39:31.260 --> 00:39:35.380
tomatoes—was inept and put these adverts
up about how wonderful this all was.

00:39:35.380 --> 00:39:41.160
And the consumer saw nothing for them, they
just saw something for Monsanto.

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:47.270
Secondly the scientists like me were inept,
because when we tried to explain it, we didn't

00:39:47.270 --> 00:39:54.020
take the rather obvious route of asking some
of the public what they thought about this

00:39:54.020 --> 00:39:55.020
issue.

00:39:55.020 --> 00:39:58.319
We simply lectured them about how this was
all wonderful.

00:39:58.319 --> 00:40:03.390
In studies that I was involved in a year later,
we did ask that question.

00:40:03.390 --> 00:40:06.931
Do you know what the most common reply was?

00:40:06.931 --> 00:40:08.810
I was really astonished.

00:40:08.810 --> 00:40:17.440
The most common reply was that they didn't
want to eat food with genes in it.

00:40:17.440 --> 00:40:22.780
And if it's funny of course, but they didn't
fully appreciate that all food has genes in

00:40:22.780 --> 00:40:23.780
it.

00:40:23.780 --> 00:40:24.780
That was a good, hearty American laugh.

00:40:24.780 --> 00:40:27.200
And we had never asked them that question.

00:40:27.200 --> 00:40:30.670
So we weren't even beginning to be on the
same table.

00:40:30.670 --> 00:40:33.540
What's the answer there?

00:40:33.540 --> 00:40:37.069
You have to talk to the public to find out
what it is you have to deal with.

00:40:37.069 --> 00:40:43.589
Can you clarify, what is the scientific consensus
on GM food?

00:40:43.589 --> 00:40:45.050
In the U.K.?

00:40:45.050 --> 00:40:46.050
In Europe?

00:40:46.050 --> 00:40:47.839
Oh, in the world.

00:40:47.839 --> 00:40:57.380
The scientific consensus is that GM is a technology
that can produce different outcomes.

00:40:57.380 --> 00:40:59.530
And just like conventional breeding.

00:40:59.530 --> 00:41:05.829
But what you should be testing is the final
product to see if it's safe and can be eaten

00:41:05.829 --> 00:41:08.020
and used safely in the environment.

00:41:08.020 --> 00:41:09.680
I mean we are GMOs.

00:41:09.680 --> 00:41:11.160
We are GMOs ourselves.

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:12.520
A human being is, right?

00:41:12.520 --> 00:41:14.650
I mean how do we get here?

00:41:14.650 --> 00:41:16.430
It's just by a slightly different process.

00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:18.230
Yes, but we don't eat ourselves.

00:41:18.230 --> 00:41:23.059
Or at least most of us don't.

00:41:23.059 --> 00:41:24.590
Maybe in New York of course.

00:41:24.590 --> 00:41:31.619
But the point is that the GM modifications,
where they're produced by normal breeding,

00:41:31.619 --> 00:41:36.559
often involving, by the way, hitting those
plants with heavy mutagenesis.

00:41:36.559 --> 00:41:39.750
So you have no idea what you've done with
them.

00:41:39.750 --> 00:41:41.960
Versus a GM modification.

00:41:41.960 --> 00:41:43.980
So that's the consensus.

00:41:43.980 --> 00:41:53.180
But the resistance is, partly we're playing
God— manipulating things—, partly anti-corporate

00:41:53.180 --> 00:41:57.660
and big companies—because they're making
it—and once again we're seeing the politics

00:41:57.660 --> 00:42:00.240
bleeding into this.

00:42:00.240 --> 00:42:05.390
And partly—and this was a tipping point
in the discussion—when the media, the newspapers

00:42:05.390 --> 00:42:10.540
in this case, looking for a good headline
so they could sell another 100,000.

00:42:10.540 --> 00:42:14.830
We had this headline in The Daily Mail—if
it is a newspaper, that's one of these rags

00:42:14.830 --> 00:42:16.109
that we have in the U.K.—calling "Frankenstein
foods".

00:42:16.109 --> 00:42:22.710
It killed the debate for 15 years.

00:42:22.710 --> 00:42:27.190
It's only now being resurrected and it is
being resurrected now.

00:42:27.190 --> 00:42:30.790
I'm on the Scientific Advice Mechanism for
the European Union.

00:42:30.790 --> 00:42:37.370
We have just produced a report on GMOs at
the request of the commission, because they

00:42:37.370 --> 00:42:41.150
are going to now re-approach this.

00:42:41.150 --> 00:42:44.410
But it took 15 more years to go by before
it could be re-addressed.

00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:47.020
Brian, what do you hear in that story?

00:42:47.020 --> 00:42:53.440
That in the U.K., —something that here we're
incredibly blasé about GM foods— that over

00:42:53.440 --> 00:43:01.770
there, that's in a sense their climate change
in the sense that much of the general public

00:43:01.770 --> 00:43:03.859
does not trust the scientists.

00:43:03.859 --> 00:43:04.859
Yeah.

00:43:04.859 --> 00:43:12.980
It's perhaps hard to predict which issues
are going to raise what sort of thought process

00:43:12.980 --> 00:43:14.339
that's going to create resistance.

00:43:14.339 --> 00:43:16.589
And I guess I don't know.

00:43:16.589 --> 00:43:22.710
Dan, is there a cultural difference where
you can put your finger on where that is the

00:43:22.710 --> 00:43:23.710
cause of it?

00:43:23.710 --> 00:43:29.190
I mean, one thing it makes you conscious of
is that there's a great deal of accident and

00:43:29.190 --> 00:43:37.690
fortuity that creates these kinds of associations
between positions on facts that, amid scientific

00:43:37.690 --> 00:43:42.859
inquiry, over space and across time, you see
just lots of changes.

00:43:42.859 --> 00:43:49.710
Which ought to make us realize too that no
technology is destined to have the kind of

00:43:49.710 --> 00:43:52.720
resistance that GM foods have in Europe.

00:43:52.720 --> 00:43:58.930
We should get on top of all the influences
that do steer technologies down one track

00:43:58.930 --> 00:44:00.420
or another.

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:04.369
Doing survey work in case studies to understand
why that happened there and why it hasn't

00:44:04.369 --> 00:44:09.359
happened here ultimately will make us smarter
about how to prevent it from happening as

00:44:09.359 --> 00:44:10.980
often as it does.

00:44:10.980 --> 00:44:18.710
But do you advocate actually looking at a
particular constituency, taking a given scientific

00:44:18.710 --> 00:44:26.660
idea, and packaging in a way that somehow
will avoid some of the pitfalls that you think

00:44:26.660 --> 00:44:28.090
are the cause of why—

00:44:28.090 --> 00:44:30.470
The HPV vaccine is a great example.

00:44:30.470 --> 00:44:36.569
Because at the very time that we were in the
midst of this very contentious political debate

00:44:36.569 --> 00:44:40.020
about whether schools should adopt it as a
mandate for—

00:44:40.020 --> 00:44:42.180
Stop again and remind us what the HPV vaccine
is.

00:44:42.180 --> 00:44:48.700
So the HPV vaccine is a vaccine that protects
women, but also men.

00:44:48.700 --> 00:44:54.059
There's no test for determining whether men
have HPV, but they clearly do contract it

00:44:54.059 --> 00:44:57.610
and disseminate it.

00:44:57.610 --> 00:45:02.780
It is an extremely common sexually transmitted
disease that causes cervical cancer.

00:45:02.780 --> 00:45:08.390
It's the only vaccine that was proposed by
the CDC for universal administration that's

00:45:08.390 --> 00:45:13.280
not on the list across the states for mandatory
school enrollment.

00:45:13.280 --> 00:45:18.359
But at the time that we were having a very
bitter contentious fight about that, we also

00:45:18.359 --> 00:45:24.059
had the HBV vaccine for Hepatitis B. Hepatitis
B is also a sexually transmitted disease and

00:45:24.059 --> 00:45:28.430
it causes actually more cancers than HPV.

00:45:28.430 --> 00:45:30.680
At the time it was being administered to adolescents.

00:45:30.680 --> 00:45:34.030
Now it's administered to infants, but there
was a catch up regime.

00:45:34.030 --> 00:45:37.280
There was no dispute about that.

00:45:37.280 --> 00:45:40.330
You could look at HPV and say, “Well, it
was bound to have conflict because you're

00:45:40.330 --> 00:45:43.750
talking about parental autonomy and about
sex.”

00:45:43.750 --> 00:45:45.320
Same thing with the HBV vaccine.

00:45:45.320 --> 00:45:50.290
The difference was that people learned about
the HBV vaccine from their doctors.

00:45:50.290 --> 00:45:56.660
They learned about HPV from watching Fox and
MSNBC go at each other because there was a

00:45:56.660 --> 00:45:58.740
legislative proposal to do this.

00:45:58.740 --> 00:46:03.320
Usually it's done by non-political health
administrators.

00:46:03.320 --> 00:46:08.530
And so we knew that was going to happen, it's
not like the evidence was rejected.

00:46:08.530 --> 00:46:15.250
But we don't have any process for assessing
how these kinds of advances in knowledge will

00:46:15.250 --> 00:46:18.069
be presented to the general public.

00:46:18.069 --> 00:46:24.990
So to Brian's question, you need to know where
are the accident's going to happen?

00:46:24.990 --> 00:46:29.490
You should be trying to simulate the conditions
under which people are going learn about these

00:46:29.490 --> 00:46:35.490
new technologies from a medical advance like
the HPV vaccine to other kinds of things like

00:46:35.490 --> 00:46:39.640
nanotechnology and the NSF— that's clearly
their philosophy.

00:46:39.640 --> 00:46:44.480
Let's learn as much as we can about the kinds
of ways of educating people that are likely

00:46:44.480 --> 00:46:47.610
to provoke this kind of polarization and the
ones that won't.

00:46:47.610 --> 00:46:49.001
John, could I just ask one more question?

00:46:49.001 --> 00:46:52.820
I don't mean to take the role, but just one
question that comes to mind here.

00:46:52.820 --> 00:47:01.440
So if we were to have a better educational
process early on, that kids really understood

00:47:01.440 --> 00:47:04.680
what science is about— I know that they
don't.

00:47:04.680 --> 00:47:09.140
There are fantastic teachers around the country,
do not get me wrong, this is not fully general.

00:47:09.140 --> 00:47:12.740
But I've spoken to so many kids around the
country whose perspective on what science

00:47:12.740 --> 00:47:17.960
is, is memorization, get the facts, know the
equation— that's all that it is.

00:47:17.960 --> 00:47:23.401
If the process that Paul was talking about
was somehow ingrained really early on, could

00:47:23.401 --> 00:47:27.300
you see that bubbling up in a way that would
mitigate the issues that you see?

00:47:27.300 --> 00:47:32.599
Actually what we do see, as I said, is that
when an issue has taken on this kind of status

00:47:32.599 --> 00:47:38.020
where the positions are badges of membership,
the people who are the most divided culturally

00:47:38.020 --> 00:47:42.150
are the ones who are highest in all of the
kinds of critical reasoning faculties that

00:47:42.150 --> 00:47:43.340
you were talking about.

00:47:43.340 --> 00:47:48.290
But does that really capture an understanding
of what science is and they know that in their

00:47:48.290 --> 00:47:49.290
bones?

00:47:49.290 --> 00:47:51.410
Do you test that in a way that really captures
that?

00:47:51.410 --> 00:47:52.410
Oh they know what science is.

00:47:52.410 --> 00:47:57.960
Oh sure, you have critical reasoning problems
about covariance detection and conditional

00:47:57.960 --> 00:47:59.690
probability, all kinds of things that people—

00:47:59.690 --> 00:48:00.690
Nah, c'mon.

00:48:00.690 --> 00:48:01.690
Yeah, c'mon.

00:48:01.690 --> 00:48:04.520
I'll administer the test to you.

00:48:04.520 --> 00:48:05.960
I want to bring in the audience for some questions.

00:48:05.960 --> 00:48:10.150
But now you see you won’t—is it your cultural
view that's causing you to dismiss this?

00:48:10.150 --> 00:48:12.970
Is it the culture of being a scientist?

00:48:12.970 --> 00:48:17.740
There’s a science—and we need a science
of science communication if we're going to

00:48:17.740 --> 00:48:18.740
address these problems.

00:48:18.740 --> 00:48:22.220
And we need to have institutions that will
use it.

00:48:22.220 --> 00:48:27.810
We're going to put some microphones up for
you to line up and ask them questions, but

00:48:27.810 --> 00:48:30.470
we did ask you the questions when you came
in.

00:48:30.470 --> 00:48:32.250
And here are the results.

00:48:32.250 --> 00:48:37.740
On the question of whether climate change
is man-made, 93.4% of you said yes.

00:48:37.740 --> 00:48:44.000
On the question of whether there is something
wrong with GMO food, 39.1% said yes.

00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:48.050
And whether you support the burial of nuclear
waste, 46% said yes.

00:48:48.050 --> 00:48:51.270
I’m curious in light of the—

00:48:51.270 --> 00:48:53.620
The burying nuclear waste?

00:48:53.620 --> 00:48:56.250
There's scientific consensus that that's OK.

00:48:56.250 --> 00:49:00.880
The National Academy of Sciences was tearing
its hair out about why the public didn't accept

00:49:00.880 --> 00:49:02.670
that long before climate change.

00:49:02.670 --> 00:49:06.770
I mean it's kind of revealing that this audience,
only 46% think that that's safe.

00:49:06.770 --> 00:49:08.640
But you're telling them that's OK.

00:49:08.640 --> 00:49:11.470
So we can poll them again and see how influential
you are.

00:49:11.470 --> 00:49:15.619
I'm telling you that the same groups that
don’t believe— that make a mistake about

00:49:15.619 --> 00:49:20.790
scientific consensus on climate change make
a mistake on the scientific consensus on deep

00:49:20.790 --> 00:49:22.400
geologic isolation of nuclear waste.

00:49:22.400 --> 00:49:27.270
If you were to phrase the nuclear burial question
in terms of, "Do you feel really strongly

00:49:27.270 --> 00:49:28.270
about your position?"

00:49:28.270 --> 00:49:30.700
I really wonder if the data would have been
different.

00:49:30.700 --> 00:49:32.140
Can we just quickly do that?

00:49:32.140 --> 00:49:35.710
Only answer if you feel really strongly about
the issue.

00:49:35.710 --> 00:49:39.640
How many people think that it's OK to deeply
bury nuclear waste?

00:49:39.640 --> 00:49:43.310
And how many people think it's not OK?

00:49:43.310 --> 00:49:46.300
It's about the same I guess.

00:49:46.300 --> 00:49:48.400
Do we have any questions yet?

00:49:48.400 --> 00:49:52.720
Because we have some coming in from online,
from YouTube from Laurie Brummett.

00:49:52.720 --> 00:49:59.580
She is asking, "What are ways in which these
panelists are working to change the public

00:49:59.580 --> 00:50:01.060
perceptions of science?

00:50:01.060 --> 00:50:06.410
Or should people with influence and power
be doing something to change the perceptions?"

00:50:06.410 --> 00:50:08.530
Why don't we take that to you, Brian.

00:50:08.530 --> 00:50:10.960
Uh, we're not doing anything.

00:50:10.960 --> 00:50:11.960
No?

00:50:11.960 --> 00:50:14.359
Yeah, just the World Science Festival.

00:50:14.359 --> 00:50:16.250
No, that's what this festival is!

00:50:16.250 --> 00:50:19.369
It's not that every scientist should be out
there talking to the public.

00:50:19.369 --> 00:50:24.359
I think there are some whose time is better
spent just in the laboratory doing their work.

00:50:24.359 --> 00:50:29.790
But those who are interested in doing it and
those who feel called to do it, I think many

00:50:29.790 --> 00:50:32.819
are going out and speaking to the public.

00:50:32.819 --> 00:50:36.770
Look, when we started this festival there
was definitely—that was 10 years ago—there

00:50:36.770 --> 00:50:41.531
was definitely some uncertainty on the part
of some scientists about, “Do I want to

00:50:41.531 --> 00:50:42.531
be part of this?

00:50:42.531 --> 00:50:44.309
Do I want to be out there in the public?”

00:50:44.309 --> 00:50:49.309
Now we have scientists all year round lobbying
to be part of the festival.

00:50:49.309 --> 00:50:54.710
So there's a change in perspective that getting
out there and talking to the public is important

00:50:54.710 --> 00:50:56.210
and really matters.

00:50:56.210 --> 00:50:59.460
And I think culturally, we even see it right
now.

00:50:59.460 --> 00:51:03.069
I mean again, let me just be personal about
it.

00:51:03.069 --> 00:51:06.670
A couple of years ago, I would not be on this
panel.

00:51:06.670 --> 00:51:12.020
The only thing I'd ever talk about is the
subject that I work on as a scientist.

00:51:12.020 --> 00:51:17.319
And now I feel moved to be a little bit more
broad because we see what's happening in the

00:51:17.319 --> 00:51:19.810
world and we have to step up and do something
about it.

00:51:19.810 --> 00:51:24.119
We even see scientists— now there's a new
group of scientists who are pledging to run

00:51:24.119 --> 00:51:25.300
for public office.

00:51:25.300 --> 00:51:27.819
It used to be “No, no, we stay away from
that.”

00:51:27.819 --> 00:51:30.140
But now we're going forward.

00:51:30.140 --> 00:51:33.089
France, I see a lot of nodding from you on
that part.

00:51:33.089 --> 00:51:35.420
I see a lot of nodding from you on Brian's
point.

00:51:35.420 --> 00:51:43.710
I mean we are embracing uncertainty a lot
more and being in uncomfortable and challenging

00:51:43.710 --> 00:51:44.780
situations.

00:51:44.780 --> 00:51:52.950
My agency spends over $60 million a year funding
what we call "advancing informal science learning".

00:51:52.950 --> 00:51:58.721
And that's money that goes to public broadcasting
stations, it goes to museums, it goes to science

00:51:58.721 --> 00:52:01.390
festivals.

00:52:01.390 --> 00:52:07.880
And that's all to try to do better about the
public communication of science.

00:52:07.880 --> 00:52:10.819
And these events are widely, widely attended.

00:52:10.819 --> 00:52:17.089
I mean some of our museums in this country
have millions of visitors a year that want

00:52:17.089 --> 00:52:18.710
to do hands-on science.

00:52:18.710 --> 00:52:20.369
Let's go to a question.

00:52:20.369 --> 00:52:21.369
Ma'am?

00:52:21.369 --> 00:52:22.369
Hi there.

00:52:22.369 --> 00:52:29.130
We have a problem with the global warming
and oil producers and people like that that

00:52:29.130 --> 00:52:32.130
are making that a big problem.

00:52:32.130 --> 00:52:37.180
But we also have nuclear weapons and that's
the other big thing that can annihilate the

00:52:37.180 --> 00:52:41.360
planet and civilization, and we wouldn't have
them if it weren't for scientists.

00:52:41.360 --> 00:52:44.369
The question is what are scientists doing
about it?

00:52:44.369 --> 00:52:48.510
How are the able—we don't have Linus Pauling
anymore, or Joseph Rotblat.

00:52:48.510 --> 00:52:52.620
Who are the scientists that will speak up
or say something about their colleagues who

00:52:52.620 --> 00:52:54.460
are making these things?

00:52:54.460 --> 00:52:57.320
I think that's a really important thing if
you want trust.

00:52:57.320 --> 00:52:58.760
That's a good question.

00:52:58.760 --> 00:53:01.150
Let's take it first to Paul.

00:53:01.150 --> 00:53:03.500
You're on the spot.

00:53:03.500 --> 00:53:07.230
Well I was a member of CND, who actually—

00:53:07.230 --> 00:53:09.010
Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament.

00:53:09.010 --> 00:53:12.480
Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament.

00:53:12.480 --> 00:53:18.510
Scientists do speak out against the use of
nuclear arms.

00:53:18.510 --> 00:53:21.160
It is, of course, a personal opinion.

00:53:21.160 --> 00:53:25.940
So it's not actually to do with science, it’s
to do with the use of them and it's just like

00:53:25.940 --> 00:53:28.780
any other member of the public.

00:53:28.780 --> 00:53:32.720
Some will speak against them and some not.

00:53:32.720 --> 00:53:38.440
If the question was—which it sort of got
close to saying— without scientific understanding,

00:53:38.440 --> 00:53:40.369
we wouldn't have nuclear weapons.

00:53:40.369 --> 00:53:43.810
And so, is that an attack on scientific understanding?

00:53:43.810 --> 00:53:49.250
Well I would say, without science we wouldn't
have many, many other things and I think that

00:53:49.250 --> 00:53:50.940
isn't the right way to think about it.

00:53:50.940 --> 00:53:52.040
So you're including nuclear power?

00:53:52.040 --> 00:53:55.450
Science gives you the knowledge, it's the
use of the knowledge... you wouldn't have

00:53:55.450 --> 00:53:57.790
nuclear power— well some don't like that
either.

00:53:57.790 --> 00:54:02.961
But science produces the knowledge, society
has to determine how the knowledge is used.

00:54:02.961 --> 00:54:04.650
Thank you for that question, very provocative.

00:54:04.650 --> 00:54:06.450
A question from over here.

00:54:06.450 --> 00:54:07.450
Hi.

00:54:07.450 --> 00:54:10.690
You guys earlier talked a little bit about
uncertainties that are inherent to— and

00:54:10.690 --> 00:54:16.270
perhaps well understood by—scientific consensus,
things like uncertainties and confidence intervals.

00:54:16.270 --> 00:54:20.549
But there have been plenty of cases in the
past where science has been just flat out

00:54:20.549 --> 00:54:25.099
wrong, whether that's because there is some
element we didn't understand or perhaps there's

00:54:25.099 --> 00:54:30.079
some methodology that we're picking up some
sort of noise.

00:54:30.079 --> 00:54:34.210
So to play devil's advocate a little bit,
I was wondering what you guys think the probability

00:54:34.210 --> 00:54:38.849
is that we're wrong about something like climate
change and whether or not we should accept

00:54:38.849 --> 00:54:40.809
some amount of humility about that.

00:54:40.809 --> 00:54:47.790
Well I emphasise that we should look upon
science as a spectrum.

00:54:47.790 --> 00:54:51.040
There is tentative knowledge and there's firmer
knowledge.

00:54:51.040 --> 00:54:53.820
And I think that's really where it is.

00:54:53.820 --> 00:54:58.440
And there’s different areas that you can
put different probabilities to.

00:54:58.440 --> 00:55:04.190
And our responsibility is really to communicate
that uncertainty and that probability.

00:55:04.190 --> 00:55:10.110
I think we should be wary of thinking when
a piece of research or an idea turns out to

00:55:10.110 --> 00:55:13.030
be wrong, as if that is just entirely negative.

00:55:13.030 --> 00:55:16.770
Because it isn't, as we touched on before.

00:55:16.770 --> 00:55:22.520
Science moves by this process of being wrong
about things, which then cleans out certain

00:55:22.520 --> 00:55:23.520
ideas.

00:55:23.520 --> 00:55:31.180
So I do think that we have to be a little
more open about the fact that there will be

00:55:31.180 --> 00:55:33.900
mistakes made in science and that's part of
the process.

00:55:33.900 --> 00:55:36.319
And I'm not talking about fraud or anything
like that.

00:55:36.319 --> 00:55:40.089
It's just that we make observations, they
may not be accurate in certain ways.

00:55:40.089 --> 00:55:43.240
We may have an idea and they don't take everything
on.

00:55:43.240 --> 00:55:45.030
But that is just the process of science.

00:55:45.030 --> 00:55:51.309
Sir, before you leave the mic, I'm curious
to know whether you found that answer satisfying.

00:55:51.309 --> 00:55:52.470
I think so.

00:55:52.470 --> 00:55:56.539
I mean I was thinking more of results such
as the recent BICEP results, which we all

00:55:56.539 --> 00:56:00.370
know were about primordial gravitational waves,
but they're very certain—

00:56:00.370 --> 00:56:04.380
Let's take that as an example because it's
much easier to speak of since it takes it

00:56:04.380 --> 00:56:05.670
out of the political realm.

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:09.319
So here is a result that was announced.

00:56:09.319 --> 00:56:11.530
Incredibly exciting that we might have detected
gravitational waves.

00:56:11.530 --> 00:56:12.530
Brian, can you catch everybody up?

00:56:12.530 --> 00:56:18.190
So a few years ago, before the recent discovery
that was announced February 11th of 2016 that

00:56:18.190 --> 00:56:22.119
we had finally discovered gravitational waves,
there was a previous announcement by a team

00:56:22.119 --> 00:56:26.859
at the South Pole that they thought they found
indirect evidence for the existence of gravitational

00:56:26.859 --> 00:56:27.859
waves.

00:56:27.859 --> 00:56:32.170
It got the world of science incredibly excited
and the data looked kind of strong.

00:56:32.170 --> 00:56:38.290
But over the course of just a few days to
weeks, scientists converged on this and began

00:56:38.290 --> 00:56:39.559
to raise issues.

00:56:39.559 --> 00:56:44.539
At Columbia, within a few days, we had a group
meeting where experts say, "Well, we don't

00:56:44.539 --> 00:56:46.349
know, it may be dust in the foreground.

00:56:46.349 --> 00:56:48.980
They didn't talk about foregrounds in the
paper."

00:56:48.980 --> 00:56:54.369
And so the community came together and within
a year, it was determined that the interpretation

00:56:54.369 --> 00:56:58.329
of the data was incorrect, that it wasn't
gravitational waves.

00:56:58.329 --> 00:57:00.369
That's beautiful!

00:57:00.369 --> 00:57:02.210
That's not something bad.

00:57:02.210 --> 00:57:06.140
That's the ability of science to self-correct.

00:57:06.140 --> 00:57:11.089
That's the beauty of it all.

00:57:11.089 --> 00:57:21.710
And just to emphasize that, when that result
came out I made a big poster picture—and

00:57:21.710 --> 00:57:27.670
dry mounted it—of the B-polarization background
map, the famous one that then turned out to

00:57:27.670 --> 00:57:32.230
be incorrect or was actually mapping dust
in our own galaxy.

00:57:32.230 --> 00:57:36.050
And I take that out and show it to visitors
every once in a while.

00:57:36.050 --> 00:57:41.851
That is one of my favorite, favorite stories
of the progress of science.

00:57:41.851 --> 00:57:46.670
Now the people that wrote the paper may feel
exactly that way, but we're talking collectively,

00:57:46.670 --> 00:57:50.069
the community we're talking about here.

00:57:50.069 --> 00:57:52.599
Over here.

00:57:52.599 --> 00:58:00.789
So recently, there was a book and also several
articles which dealt with the reproducibility

00:58:00.789 --> 00:58:07.349
crisis in biomedical research and I'm wondering
whether you, the panel, think that that is

00:58:07.349 --> 00:58:16.299
something which is going to hurt science in
the public perception generally?

00:58:16.299 --> 00:58:18.690
And what we should do about that?

00:58:18.690 --> 00:58:22.609
I think I've got to catch that one, haven't
I?

00:58:22.609 --> 00:58:26.010
Yes, reproducibility, particularly in medical
science actually.

00:58:26.010 --> 00:58:28.520
I got a few comments to make.

00:58:28.520 --> 00:58:35.650
The first thing is that biological research
is made with biological material.

00:58:35.650 --> 00:58:44.500
Biological material is often very variable
and some of this reproducibility issue is

00:58:44.500 --> 00:58:50.650
around the fact that you're not always working
with exactly the same things because of their

00:58:50.650 --> 00:58:51.670
biological variability.

00:58:51.670 --> 00:59:00.100
A second aspect to this is that some of the
the techniques being used are very sophisticated

00:59:00.100 --> 00:59:04.440
and complicated, and can be difficult to actually
reproduce.

00:59:04.440 --> 00:59:11.069
There is something that's growing up, that
if somebody says ‘this’ and then somebody

00:59:11.069 --> 00:59:14.690
doesn't get the same result, this person is
always wrong.

00:59:14.690 --> 00:59:17.859
That isn't actually necessarily the case.

00:59:17.859 --> 00:59:19.640
This person might be wrong.

00:59:19.640 --> 00:59:21.640
It is part of actually the process.

00:59:21.640 --> 00:59:26.319
Then there's a third aspect of it, which is
the one that is spoken about most, which is

00:59:26.319 --> 00:59:30.270
that people are just making it up and it's
fake science and they're trying to make money

00:59:30.270 --> 00:59:32.109
and they're driving things through.

00:59:32.109 --> 00:59:37.230
And there is no question in my mind that there
is some pressures in this direction: pressures

00:59:37.230 --> 00:59:42.750
for people's careers, to get interesting results,
to get interesting papers, sometimes just

00:59:42.750 --> 00:59:44.040
commercial interests.

00:59:44.040 --> 00:59:53.809
And my view about that is that we simply have
to have a good culture in scientific research

00:59:53.809 --> 00:59:56.710
where that is simply not acceptable.

00:59:56.710 --> 01:00:01.700
It's acceptable to make genuine mistakes because
that will happen sometimes.

01:00:01.700 --> 01:00:05.730
It is not acceptable to be sloppy in the work.

01:00:05.730 --> 01:00:08.010
It's not acceptable to be fraudulent.

01:00:08.010 --> 01:00:14.641
I sum it up: science is a high calling, and
I'm not sure we emphasize enough, it is a

01:00:14.641 --> 01:00:17.520
high calling and we have to hold ourselves
to high standards.

01:00:17.520 --> 01:00:18.520
Thank you very much everybody.

01:00:18.520 --> 01:00:18.521
Goodnight!

